This is code that was found or located on old backup tapes and who knows
where. Some of this is pure F77 that needs a lot of care to cleanup.

The file CHESS.F is the original code that was written between
the years 1975 up to 1978. It is not well formatted and seems
to have tab chars in the wrong places as well as continuation
chars in the wrong columns.

At least on a decent IBM POWER9 server with decent compilers
we may do --> f77 -qctyplss -o chess  CHESS_REV1.f

Also there seems to be some confusion where modern folks are
thinking this software is using modern chess notation.

   *******************************************************
   *                                                     *
   *  I T    I S    N O T    D O I N G    M O D E R N    *
   *                                                     *
   *              C H E S S    N O T A T I O N           *
   *                                                     *
   *******************************************************

So just use the coordinates that the software displays and you
may be okay. Maybe. No promise.

Even more confusing is that you may move first or the software
may move first and you end up playing white pieces regardless.

Good luck figureing that out!


--- here is a compile on an IBM POWER9 with IBM F77 compiler --------

dax$ f77 -qctyplss -o chess  CHESS_REV1.f
